\title{Tightness of the Unit-Shift Bound for Euler's Totient Function:\\
A Constructive and Verified Skeleton}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\varphi$ denote Euler's totient function and define the unit-shift collision
counting function $\Sphi(x) = \#\{\, n \le x : \varphi(n) = \varphi(n+1)\,\}$. A
theorem of Graham, Holt, and Pomerance gives the upper bound $\Sphi(x) \ll
x\exp\{-(\tfrac12 - o(1))\sqrt{\log x\,\log_2 x}\}$, with the companion tightness
statement $\Sphi(x) \ge C\,x\exp\{-(\tfrac12 + o(1))\sqrt{\log x\,\log_2 x}\}$ for a
constant $C>0$ and all large $x$. The full asymptotic is analytic, but its engine
is constructive: one builds many integers $n$ with $\varphi(n)=\varphi(n+1)$ and
counts them. We formalize that engine. We (i) certify a family of explicit
collisions via coprime multiplicativity of $\varphi$; (ii) prove a counting
transfer theorem turning any finite verified witness set into a lower bound on
$\Sphi$; (iii) derive $6\le \Sphi(194)$ and $10 \le \Sphi(975)$; (iv) prove
non-saturation $\Sphi(x)<x$ for $x\ge 2$; and (v) prove that every collision value
with $n\ge 3$ is even. Each collision is verified by factoring $n$ and $n+1$ into
coprime prime powers, exposing the ``power-of-two versus product-of-small-odd-primes''
balancing that drives the lower-bound construction. All results are machine-checked.
\end{abstract}

